# Baltimore oriole

The Baltimore oriole plaits a deep pouch for its eggs and slings it from the tip
of a slender elm or maple limb, where it swings free with nothing beneath it and
few climbing predators will follow. The female knots the first strands around a
forked twig and twines plant fibre, horsehair and shreds of bark downward until
the chamber holds its shape, sometimes sixteen centimetres deep. The species is
a brilliant orange and black icterid of eastern North America, arriving from the
tropics in late spring. See [[icteridae]].
